= Prieto and Caballero

From prison and exile, the two great figures of the <span style="color: #c00000;">**PSOE**</span> pull the party in opposite directions. Indalecio Prieto argues that October was a catastrophe and that the only path is a broad electoral alliance with the republicans to win back the Republic through the ballot. Francisco Largo Caballero - the "Spanish Lenin" to his adoring youth - has concluded the opposite: that bourgeois democracy is a trap, and that the workers must prepare for the dictatorship of the proletariat.

The <span style="color: #8a0303;">Socialist Youth</span> chant Caballero's name; the union machine and the moderates hold with Prieto. The party will hold together for the elections - both men want the prisoners freed - but the fracture running through it is the same one that runs through the whole European left in these years.

[? if left_strength >= reformist_strength : For now the Caballerista tide is running strongest, and the talk in the locals is of revolution, not coalition. ?][? if reformist_strength > left_strength : For now Prieto's caution prevails, and the party bends toward the republican alliance. ?]
